{"step_index":395,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-08T21:56:37Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-09T05:56:37+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-09T05:56:38+08:00\nShowing lines 135-180 of 454\n135:   projectId: \"sir-jasper-quiz\",\n136:   storageBucket: \"sir-jasper-quiz.firebasestorage.app\",\n137:   messagingSenderId: \"932129654171\",\n138:   appId: \"1:932129654171:web:793aa17dd1f130a62f4d3e\"\n139: };\n140: \n141: const defaultQuiz = {\n142:   title: \"C Quiz: Switch Case\",\n143:   description: \"20 items • 20 seconds per question • Do not switch tabs or windows.\",\n144:   settings: {\n145:     timePerQuestion: 20,\n146:     enableTimer: true,\n147:     enableTabViolation: true,\n148:     tabViolationPenalty: 1,\n149:     randomizeQuestions: true,\n150:     randomizeAnswers: true\n151:   },\n152:   questions: [\n153:     { q: \"Which C statement selects one of many code blocks to run based on the value of an expression?\", correct: \"switch\", wrongs: [\"for\", \"while\", \"goto\"], explanation: \"switch compares one expression against a list of constant case labels and runs the matching block.\" },\n154:     { q: \"Which type of expression can be used as the controlling expression of a switch in C?\", correct: \"int or char\", wrongs: [\"float\", \"double\", \"string (char*)\"], explanation: \"switch works with integral types such as int and char, not floating-point values or strings.\" },\n155:     { q: \"Which keyword prevents execution from falling through to the next case?\", correct: \"break\", wrongs: [\"continue\", \"return 0\", \"exit\"], explanation: \"break leaves the switch block immediately after the matching case finishes.\" },\n156:     { q: \"When does the default label run?\", correct: \"When no case label matches\", wrongs: [\"Always, before the cases\", \"Only when the first case fails\", \"Only if break is missing\"], explanation: \"default is the fallback block used when no case matches the expression.\" },\n157:     { q: \"What is the output?\\nint x = 2;\\nswitch (x) {\\n  case 1: printf(\\\"A\\\");\\n  case 2: printf(\\\"B\\\");\\n  case 3: printf(\\\"C\\\");\\n}\", correct: \"BC\", wrongs: [\"B\", \"C\", \"ABC\"], explanation: \"No break statements, so execution starts at case 2 and falls through to case 3.\" },\n158:     { q: \"What is the output?\\nint x = 3;\\nswitch (x) {\\n  case 1: printf(\\\"1\\\"); break;\\n  default: printf(\\\"D\\\");\\n}\", correct: \"D\", wrongs: [\"1\", \"1D\", \"Nothing is printed\"], explanation: \"x is 3, no case matches, so default runs.\" },\n159:     { q: \"Is the default label required in a switch statement?\", correct: \"No, it is optional\", wrongs: [\"Yes, always\", \"Yes, but only with char\", \"Only if there is no break\"], explanation: \"default is optional; without it, nothing runs when no case matches.\" },\n160:     { q: \"Which case label is valid in C?\", correct: \"case 'A':\", wrongs: [\"case x:  (x is a variable)\", \"case 1.5:\", \"case \\\"A\\\":\"], explanation: \"Case labels must be integer or character constants. Variables, floats and strings are not allowed.\" },\n161:     { q: \"What is the output?\\nchar c = 'b';\\nswitch (c) {\\n  case 'a': printf(\\\"1\\\"); break;\\n  case 'b': printf(\\\"2\\\");\\n  case 'c': printf(\\\"3\\\"); break;\\n}\", correct: \"23\", wrongs: [\"2\", \"3\", \"123\"], explanation: \"case 'b' matches and prints 2, has no break, then falls into case 'c' and prints 3 before break.\" },\n162:     { q: \"What happens if two case labels in the same switch have the same constant value?\", correct: \"Compile-time error\", wrongs: [\"The first one runs\", \"The last one runs\", \"Both run\"], explanation: \"Duplicate case values are not allowed and the compiler reports an error.\" },\n163:     { q: \"Where can the default label be placed inside a switch?\", correct: \"Anywhere inside the switch\", wrongs: [\"Only at the end\", \"Only at the beginning\", \"Only after a break\"], explanation: \"default may appear in any position; it is used only when no case matches.\" },\n164:     { q: \"What is the output when x = 1?\\nswitch (x) {\\n  case 1:\\n  case 2: printf(\\\"Low\\\"); break;\\n  case 3: printf(\\\"High\\\");\\n}\", correct: \"Low\", wrongs: [\"High\", \"LowHigh\", \"Nothing is printed\"], explanation: \"Stacked labels share one block, so case 1 falls into case 2's code, which prints Low and breaks.\" },\n165:     { q: \"Which is NOT allowed as the controlling expression of a switch in C?\", correct: \"A float variable, e.g. switch(f)\", wrongs: [\"An int variable, e.g. switch(x)\", \"A char variable, e.g. switch(c)\", \"An expression, e.g. switch(x % 3)\"], explanation: \"The controlling expression must be an integer type; float values are not allowed.\" },\n166:     { q: \"Which statement about case labels is true?\", correct: \"They must be constant expressions\", wrongs: [\"They can be any variable\", \"They can be float values\", \"They can be strings\"], explanation: \"The compiler needs constant integer or character values to build the jump table.\" },\n167:     { q: \"What is the output?\\nint x = 5;\\nswitch (x % 2) {\\n  case 0: printf(\\\"Even\\\"); break;\\n  case 1: printf(\\\"Odd\\\"); break;\\n}\", correct: \"Odd\", wrongs: [\"Even\", \"EvenOdd\", \"Nothing is printed\"], explanation: \"5 % 2 is 1, so case 1 runs.\" },\n168:     { q: \"What is the output?\\nint x = 1;\\nswitch (x) {\\n  default: printf(\\\"D\\\");\\n  case 1: printf(\\\"1\\\"); break;\\n  case 2: printf(\\\"2\\\");\\n}\", correct: \"1\", wrongs: [\"D1\", \"D\", \"12\"], explanation: \"x matches case 1 directly, so default is skipped. Only 1 is printed, then break.\" },\n169:     { q: \"When is switch preferred over an if-else if chain?\", correct: \"Comparing one variable against several constant values\", wrongs: [\"Testing ranges like x > 10\", \"Comparing strings\", \"Evaluating floating-point conditions\"], explanation: \"switch is designed for equality checks of one integral value against constants. It cannot test ranges.\" },\n170:     { q: \"What is the value printed?\\nint a = 1;\\nswitch (a) {\\n  case 1: a++;\\n  case 2: a += 2; break;\\n  case 3: a = 10;\\n}\\nprintf(\\\"%d\\\", a);\", correct: \"4\", wrongs: [\"3\", \"2\", \"10\"], explanation: \"case 1 makes a = 2, falls into case 2 so a = 4, then break.\" },\n171:     { q: \"Which is the correct switch syntax?\", correct: \"switch (x) { case 1: printf(\\\"One\\\"); break; }\", wrongs: [\"switch x { case 1: printf(\\\"One\\\"); break; }\", \"switch (x) { case 1 printf(\\\"One\\\"); break; }\", \"switch (x) { case(1) = printf(\\\"One\\\"); }\"], explanation: \"The expression goes in parentheses and each case label ends with a colon.\" },\n172:     { q: \"Can a switch statement be nested inside another switch statement in C?\", correct: \"Yes\", wrongs: [\"No\", \"Only inside default\", \"Only with goto\"], explanation: \"Switch statements can be nested; each break only exits the innermost switch.\" }\n173:   ]\n174: };\n175: \n176: let db = null;\n177: try {\n178:   firebase.initializeApp(cfg);\n179:   db = firebase.firestore();\n180: } catch (e) {}"}
